WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.368 --> 00:00:02.736 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I go out on my dock at
night, up north where I am now

00:00:02.736 --> 00:00:04.137
and it's dark

00:00:04.137 --> 00:00:07.107 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the expanse of the
heavens is laid out above me.

00:00:07.107 --> 00:00:12.112
And I look up, well, I'm not
thinking religious thoughts.

00:00:13.246 --> 00:00:16.016
I'm confronting the
infinite itself,

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:18.318
and that moves you inside.

00:00:18.318 --> 00:00:20.754
And I would say it even
moves you to imitation

00:00:20.754 --> 00:00:21.688
because you know what it means

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:23.690
to have your head in the stars.

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.191
Right?

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:27.560
It means to be oriented up in
the highest possible manner.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.562 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so, we're so
compelled to imitate,

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:32.232 align:start size:97% position:3%
again, at an instinctual
level that we can even imitate

00:00:32.232 --> 00:00:35.101 align:start position:0%
the infinite that we see a
spread above us in the night sky

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:37.570
and that's a religious calling.

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.873 align:start size:97% position:3%
And all this talk about
whether you believe in God,

00:00:39.873 --> 00:00:41.241
it's all propositionalised.

00:00:41.241 --> 00:00:42.208
It's like...

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:44.577
Well, it misses the point.

00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:46.479
That's why I don't ever
answer when people say,

00:00:46.479 --> 00:00:47.747
"Do you believe in God?"

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:49.582
I say, "What do you
mean by believe?"

00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:50.950
And they think, "Well,
you know what I mean?"

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.119 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's like, no, you don't
know what you mean.

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:54.621
You have a bunch of assumptions

00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:56.256
that you don't even recognize

00:00:56.256 --> 00:00:58.124 align:start size:84% position:16%
that you're peppering
the question with

00:00:58.124 --> 00:00:59.959
to force me into a box

00:00:59.959 --> 00:01:03.096
to deliver the kind of answer
that your question demands

00:01:03.096 --> 00:01:05.165
given the way you phrased it.

00:01:05.165 --> 00:01:07.400
It's like, no.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.969
(upbeat music)

00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:15.675 line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin,

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:16.943 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
and joining me
today is an author,

00:01:16.943 --> 00:01:19.212 line:0%
a lecturer, a psychologist,

00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:22.315 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
and the world's
preeminent lobster expert,

00:01:22.315 --> 00:01:24.451
Jordan Peterson, my old friend.

00:01:24.451 --> 00:01:26.786
Welcome back to
the Rubin Report.

00:01:26.786 --> 00:01:28.021
- Hi, Dave.

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:30.290
So good to see you and
to be doing this again.

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:31.791
- All right, here we go again.

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:33.960
We've done this a
couple of times.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.895 align:start size:97% position:3%
I thought we'll do it a
little differently this time

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:38.565
instead of just me tossing
you up for some questions.

00:01:38.565 --> 00:01:40.433
If we did a little
kind of push and pull

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:42.602 align:start size:84% position:16%
and churn through
some of these ideas,

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:46.673
but I guess where we start
first is, you're in Toronto.

00:01:46.673 --> 00:01:48.475
I'm in Los Angeles.

00:01:48.475 --> 00:01:51.778
We live in places that are
seemingly really out of touch

00:01:51.778 --> 00:01:53.580 align:start size:84% position:16%
with a lot of the
things we talk about.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.615
People ask me about that a lot.

00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:57.884
How's life in Toronto right now.

00:01:59.352 --> 00:02:02.722 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Well, in relationship
to the COVID restrictions,

00:02:02.722 --> 00:02:04.824 line:0%
I talked to a senior advisor

00:02:04.824 --> 00:02:07.760 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
to one of the
provincial governments
a couple of weeks ago.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:10.130 align:start size:88% position:13%
He told me flat out that
the COVID policy here

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.098 align:start size:84% position:16%
is driven by nothing
but opinion polls

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:14.200 align:start size:94% position:6%
related to the popularity
of the government,

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:18.505
no science, no end game
in sight, no real plan.

00:02:18.505 --> 00:02:20.006
And so what that means is that

00:02:21.374 --> 00:02:23.710 align:start size:97% position:3%
part of the population
that's most afraid of COVID,

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:25.178
I know it's what,

00:02:25.178 --> 00:02:28.114
50% of Democrats believe
that you have a 50% shot at

00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:29.549
getting hospitalized
if you catch COVID

00:02:29.549 --> 00:02:32.152
and 25% of Republicans.

00:02:32.152 --> 00:02:34.087
And so I suspect it's
similar in Canada.

00:02:34.087 --> 00:02:36.956
And so, policy is being driven
by people who are more afraid

00:02:36.956 --> 00:02:38.358
than they should be,

00:02:38.358 --> 00:02:41.327 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it was a very
disheartening conversation

00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:42.562
because I trust this guy

00:02:42.562 --> 00:02:44.731
and he knows what
he's talking about.

00:02:44.731 --> 00:02:45.565
And so,

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:49.335
I wouldn't say I'm cynical
about governments exactly

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:51.938
because cynicism is,

00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:56.910 align:start position:0%
it's a cheap shortcut to
approximating wisdom, let's say,

00:02:58.278 --> 00:03:00.280 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you have to be judicious
in your criticisms.

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.382
But I still found that
extremely disheartening

00:03:02.382 --> 00:03:05.518
because I thought at
least policies that
I don't agree with,

00:03:05.518 --> 00:03:08.588
the restrictive policies
were at least driven by

00:03:08.588 --> 00:03:10.456
something remotely resembling

00:03:10.456 --> 00:03:11.958
a scientifically informed plan

00:03:11.958 --> 00:03:15.295 align:start size:88% position:13%
and he was irate at
what had been happening,

00:03:15.295 --> 00:03:17.463
enough to consider resigning.

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:19.866
So it's pretty appalling.

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:22.735 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So what does that tell
you at a psychological level

00:03:22.735 --> 00:03:24.470
about fear in general?

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:25.939
If this isn't driven by science

00:03:25.939 --> 00:03:27.540 align:start size:84% position:16%
and people hear
things on the media

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.642 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then suddenly
they say, "Okay, yes.

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:31.010
please lock me in my house.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.612
Please keep me in
a mask forever.

00:03:32.612 --> 00:03:35.515
Please keep me not going to
holidays with my family."

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:37.350
Just psychologically,

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:40.019 align:start size:97% position:3%
has that unearthed anything
that maybe you thought

00:03:40.019 --> 00:03:40.820
wasn't as intense?

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:41.788
- Well, I think

00:03:42.989 --> 00:03:46.092
the thing that surprised
me the most probably was

00:03:46.092 --> 00:03:50.997
how rapidly we stampeded to
imitate a totalitarian state

00:03:50.997 --> 00:03:53.533
in the immediate aftermath
of the release of COVID.

00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:56.302
Now, if you think it
through a little bit,

00:03:56.302 --> 00:03:59.739 align:start size:97% position:3%
no one really knew how serious
the virus was going to be

00:03:59.739 --> 00:04:02.375
and so it was an unknown threat.

00:04:02.375 --> 00:04:05.545
And so you can imagine a herd
of animals or a school of fish

00:04:05.545 --> 00:04:08.781
for that matter, because this
kind of phenomena is universal

00:04:08.781 --> 00:04:11.084
throughout the animal kingdom.

00:04:11.084 --> 00:04:15.255
The cost of overreacting to
a threat is generally minimal

00:04:15.255 --> 00:04:18.291
compared to under-reacting
to a real threat, right?

00:04:18.291 --> 00:04:20.593 align:start size:97% position:3%
Overreacting to a
hypothetical threat is cheap

00:04:20.593 --> 00:04:22.862 align:start size:94% position:6%
compared to under-reacting
to an actual threat

00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:24.530
'cause if you under
react, you can die.

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:25.765
Whereas if you overreact,

00:04:25.765 --> 00:04:27.567 align:start size:84% position:16%
you generally just get
tired for a minute.

00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:29.002
So a hurdle stampede,

00:04:29.002 --> 00:04:32.505 align:start position:0%
because the most neurotic
member of the herd jumps first,

00:04:32.505 --> 00:04:34.674 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then they'll
instantly follow them

00:04:34.674 --> 00:04:35.942
and that's kind of what we did

00:04:35.942 --> 00:04:38.478
in the early stages
of the pandemic.

00:04:38.478 --> 00:04:40.013
The Chinese acted first.

00:04:40.013 --> 00:04:41.514
Now, unfortunately,

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:44.083 align:start size:94% position:6%
they are a totalitarian
state and we all followed.

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:45.752 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that's excusable
in some sense

00:04:45.752 --> 00:04:49.022 align:start size:97% position:3%
because we didn't know what
sort of threat we were facing.

00:04:49.022 --> 00:04:51.524
But then the breakdown...

00:04:51.524 --> 00:04:52.992
It's really appalling in Canada,

00:04:52.992 --> 00:04:57.130 align:start size:97% position:3%
the breakdown of our rights,
let's say, for mobility,

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:59.432 align:start size:81% position:19%
for freedom of
speech, et cetera,

00:04:59.432 --> 00:05:03.102
it's particular to grating to
me in the Canadian context.

00:05:03.102 --> 00:05:06.072
Because way back in the '80s,

00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:07.340
Prime Minister, Pierre Trudeau,

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:09.542
our current Prime
Minister's father

00:05:09.542 --> 00:05:11.611
who was quite an intellectual,

00:05:11.611 --> 00:05:14.013
he was very enamored
of French civil law,

00:05:14.013 --> 00:05:16.249
and he grafted a bill
of rights on top of the

00:05:16.249 --> 00:05:18.718 align:start size:94% position:6%
Canadian federal common
law structure, essentially

00:05:18.718 --> 00:05:20.019
and I didn't like that at all.

00:05:20.019 --> 00:05:21.521
I thought that was
a bastardization

00:05:21.521 --> 00:05:23.956
of a great English
common law tradition.

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:25.525
I'm not a fan of bills of rights

00:05:25.525 --> 00:05:27.660 align:start size:94% position:6%
because they're predicated
on the idea that

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:29.062
you have a finite set of rights

00:05:29.062 --> 00:05:31.297 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that the social
contract awards them to you

00:05:31.297 --> 00:05:33.800
or the government and I
think that's backwards.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:37.003
It's like, you have all
the rights there are,

00:05:37.003 --> 00:05:39.405
except those that are
expressly forbidden

00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:42.475
by detailed legislation
that mostly was generated

00:05:42.475 --> 00:05:45.645
from the bottom up, that's the
English common law tradition.

00:05:45.645 --> 00:05:47.613 align:start size:97% position:3%
Anyways, everybody celebrated
the bill of rights,

00:05:47.613 --> 00:05:49.148
you're protected,

00:05:49.148 --> 00:05:52.518
Oh my God, all our rights
are protected now forever.

00:05:52.518 --> 00:05:56.723
It was Pierre Trudeau's major
accomplishment hypothetically.

00:05:56.723 --> 00:05:59.025 align:start size:81% position:19%
Now we have his
son and it's like,

00:05:59.025 --> 00:06:01.728
"Yeah, what's it good for?"

00:06:01.728 --> 00:06:03.596
My father isn't vaccinated.

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:05.098
He decided not to partly because

00:06:05.098 --> 00:06:06.899
they were telling him he had to,

00:06:06.899 --> 00:06:09.435
and he has his other reasons.

00:06:09.435 --> 00:06:11.104
I have family members
who aren't vaccinated

00:06:11.104 --> 00:06:13.573
for health reasons who
also had COVID twice

00:06:13.573 --> 00:06:16.676 align:start size:97% position:3%
and didn't really feel they
needed to be vaccinated again.

00:06:16.676 --> 00:06:20.413 align:start size:88% position:13%
In any case, it's
extraordinarily annoying

00:06:20.413 --> 00:06:23.649 align:start size:94% position:6%
to see this happening
and to then find out that

00:06:23.649 --> 00:06:26.219
there's nothing behind it except

00:06:26.219 --> 00:06:31.124
the most instrumental and
cowardly random polling

00:06:32.692 --> 00:06:35.695 align:start size:94% position:6%
is extremely disheartening
and also maddening

00:06:35.695 --> 00:06:38.865 align:start size:88% position:13%
and also angering all
of those things at once.

00:06:38.865 --> 00:06:40.099
- Do you think it's possible

00:06:40.099 --> 00:06:42.135
that the polling is
out of whack too?

00:06:42.135 --> 00:06:43.603
- Of course it is.

00:06:43.603 --> 00:06:44.871 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I mean, you've been doing
polling forever, right?

00:06:44.871 --> 00:06:46.072
I mean, this is one of
the things that, yeah.

00:06:46.072 --> 00:06:48.441
- Of course it's out
of whack, Jesus Christ.

00:06:48.441 --> 00:06:51.210
I mean, how do you get
accurate polling data?

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:52.812
Well, okay, over what timeframe?

00:06:52.812 --> 00:06:53.913
You're gonna poll every day?

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:55.381
How about every hour?

00:06:55.381 --> 00:06:58.117 align:start size:97% position:3%
How about every minute,
every week, every two weeks?

00:06:58.117 --> 00:07:00.720 align:start size:97% position:3%
Who answers the phone calls
when you do the polls?

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.655 align:start size:84% position:16%
How did you construct
the questions?

00:07:02.655 --> 00:07:05.224 align:start size:94% position:6%
Are you eliciting a
particular kind of answer

00:07:05.224 --> 00:07:06.859 align:start size:84% position:16%
because you paid
insufficient attention

00:07:06.859 --> 00:07:08.761 align:start size:84% position:16%
to the way the
question was posed?

00:07:08.761 --> 00:07:10.463
Et cetera, et cetera.

00:07:10.463 --> 00:07:13.966 align:start size:84% position:16%
So it's not guidance
from the polls.

00:07:13.966 --> 00:07:16.769 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's a quasi
informed random walk

00:07:16.769 --> 00:07:19.105 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is a lot easier
than thinking it through.

00:07:19.105 --> 00:07:21.641
But to see that happening

00:07:21.641 --> 00:07:25.845
in spite of the much vaunted
protection for our rights,

00:07:25.845 --> 00:07:27.313 align:start size:84% position:16%
Canadians who aren't
vaccinated now

00:07:27.313 --> 00:07:29.015
cannot leave the country.

00:07:29.015 --> 00:07:30.416 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah.
- Like what the hell?

00:07:30.416 --> 00:07:31.484
Why is that?

00:07:31.484 --> 00:07:32.919
And I'm...

00:07:32.919 --> 00:07:36.222 align:start position:0%
Look, I got vaccinated and
people took me to task for that

00:07:36.222 --> 00:07:38.357 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I thought, "All right,
I'll get the vaccine.

00:07:38.357 --> 00:07:39.859
Here's the deal, guys.

00:07:39.859 --> 00:07:44.697
I'll get the vaccine, you
fucking leave me alone."

00:07:44.697 --> 00:07:45.798
And did that work?

00:07:45.798 --> 00:07:46.866
No.

00:07:46.866 --> 00:07:48.501
So, stupid me.

00:07:49.402 --> 00:07:50.870
That's how I feel about it.

00:07:52.305 --> 00:07:53.906 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's like, well now I have
to get tested for COVID

00:07:53.906 --> 00:07:55.141
when I come back into Canada.

00:07:55.141 --> 00:07:57.643
I have to get tested
before I leave Canada.

00:07:57.643 --> 00:08:00.646
Now, that might be
the latter issue.

00:08:00.646 --> 00:08:02.782 align:start size:81% position:19%
That's an issue
with the Americans

00:08:02.782 --> 00:08:05.051 align:start size:88% position:13%
and so that's outside
of the Canadian purview,

00:08:05.051 --> 00:08:07.320
but the restrictions
to get back into Canada

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:08.521
are even more stringent.

00:08:08.521 --> 00:08:09.956
It's like, "Why get
the vaccine then

00:08:09.956 --> 00:08:12.291
if you're not going
to leave me alone?"

00:08:12.291 --> 00:08:14.794 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I don't think the
evidence that unvaccinated

00:08:14.794 --> 00:08:18.498
or that vaccinated people are
less contagious, let's say,

00:08:18.498 --> 00:08:20.032
I don't think it's
very compelling.

00:08:20.032 --> 00:08:21.501
- [Rubin] Yeah.

00:08:21.501 --> 00:08:26.138 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So why are the unvaccinated
all of a sudden a danger?

00:08:27.306 --> 00:08:28.241
And I certainly don't
understand the push

00:08:28.241 --> 00:08:29.342
to get children vaccinated.

00:08:33.079 --> 00:08:34.780 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think that's
absolutely reprehensible

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:36.182
and I also can't figure out.

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:39.285
Norman Doidge wrote
a piece in Tablet

00:08:39.285 --> 00:08:40.720
called "Needle Points."

00:08:40.720 --> 00:08:42.655
And one of the things
he pointed out was that

00:08:42.655 --> 00:08:46.492 align:start size:97% position:3%
if you take the top 25
least trusted institutions,

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:49.595
in that group,

00:08:49.595 --> 00:08:53.799 align:start position:0%
the most distrusted
institutions include Big Pharma

00:08:54.634 --> 00:08:55.568
and for good reason.

00:08:55.568 --> 00:08:57.036
And he details out the lawsuits

00:08:57.036 --> 00:08:58.504
that Big Pharma has had to pay

00:08:58.504 --> 00:09:01.307 align:start size:84% position:16%
because of misbehavior
on their part,

00:09:01.307 --> 00:09:05.111 align:start position:0%
broadly speaking multiple
companies over the last 20 years

00:09:05.111 --> 00:09:07.747 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they're the biggest
lawsuits in American history,

00:09:07.747 --> 00:09:09.148
which is really saying something

00:09:09.148 --> 00:09:11.417
because your court
system is set up

00:09:11.417 --> 00:09:14.453
so that big lawsuits
are really possible.

00:09:14.453 --> 00:09:15.655
- [Rubin] Right.

00:09:15.655 --> 00:09:17.223 align:start size:81% position:19%
- And so I see
that the leftists,

00:09:17.223 --> 00:09:19.659 align:start position:0%
all of a sudden it's like,
"Big Pharma, yeah, trust them."

00:09:19.659 --> 00:09:20.693
It's like, what?

00:09:20.693 --> 00:09:21.694
What?

00:09:21.694 --> 00:09:22.562
Really?

00:09:22.562 --> 00:09:25.164
You guys, this is...

00:09:25.164 --> 00:09:27.133
I don't understand that at all.

00:09:27.133 --> 00:09:28.067
And psychologically...

00:09:28.067 --> 00:09:29.168
So what's going on here?

00:09:29.168 --> 00:09:30.570
It's like, well,

00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:32.038
I think the underlying

00:09:33.406 --> 00:09:34.240
phenomenon is something like

00:09:36.175 --> 00:09:39.579
well, as long as it's
for health and safety,

00:09:39.579 --> 00:09:41.547
it's always good.

00:09:41.547 --> 00:09:44.183 align:start size:84% position:16%
And not to get
conspiratorial here,

00:09:44.183 --> 00:09:45.885 align:start size:88% position:13%
but the same damn thing
is going to happen

00:09:45.885 --> 00:09:47.486
with the climate change push.

00:09:47.486 --> 00:09:48.588
- [Rubin] Absolutely.

00:09:48.588 --> 00:09:49.822 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Absolutely.
- They're already re...

00:09:49.822 --> 00:09:51.290
It's already being
reconfigured as,

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:53.392 align:start size:97% position:3%
well, it's the biggest public
health issue of our time.

00:09:53.392 --> 00:09:54.994
It's like, no, I don't think so.

00:09:54.994 --> 00:09:57.797 align:start position:0%
I think overreaching bureaucrats
are the biggest health,

00:09:58.931 --> 00:10:00.166
What would you say?

00:10:00.166 --> 00:10:01.901
The biggest challenge to
our health of our time

00:10:04.103 --> 00:10:07.406 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I think that's especially
true of the COP26 bunch.

00:10:07.406 --> 00:10:10.209 align:start size:97% position:3%
The Chinese just announced
today, I believe it was today,

00:10:10.209 --> 00:10:13.079
they're building 150
new nuclear actors.

00:10:13.079 --> 00:10:16.949
Like not one, 150,

00:10:16.949 --> 00:10:19.285
and we can't get our act
together in the States,

00:10:19.285 --> 00:10:21.420 align:start size:88% position:13%
and Canada for that
matter to build like one

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:23.389 align:start size:94% position:6%
and in California,
they're gonna shut, what?

00:10:23.389 --> 00:10:25.124
10% of the power there down.

00:10:25.124 --> 00:10:26.359
It's like, no nuclear.

00:10:26.359 --> 00:10:27.860 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well, what are we
gonna replace it with?

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:29.095
Wind.

00:10:29.095 --> 00:10:30.529
Yeah, well only if
we put windmills

00:10:30.529 --> 00:10:33.733 align:start size:97% position:3%
in front of the politicians
pushing for wind.

00:10:33.733 --> 00:10:35.234 align:start size:88% position:13%
Can you imagine how
much wind it would take

00:10:35.234 --> 00:10:37.103
to blow your car down the road?

00:10:38.704 --> 00:10:40.272
So it's so...

00:10:41.841 --> 00:10:43.309
And so if those people

00:10:43.309 --> 00:10:44.977
at the climate change
summit were serious,

00:10:44.977 --> 00:10:46.445
what they would be

00:10:46.445 --> 00:10:48.147 align:start size:94% position:6%
and we could be very
very specific about this.

00:10:48.147 --> 00:10:51.117
Here's what you do
if you're a leftist

00:10:51.117 --> 00:10:53.386 align:start size:84% position:16%
who's concerned
about the environment

00:10:53.386 --> 00:10:56.722 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the poor
simultaneously, okay?

00:10:56.722 --> 00:11:01.460 align:start size:97% position:3%
You may power energy as cheap
as possible for everyone

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:03.696
because there's no difference
between energy and wealth,

00:11:03.696 --> 00:11:07.099 align:start size:88% position:13%
and because rich people
can't hoard energy.

00:11:07.099 --> 00:11:08.634
It's not a Prieto
problem, right?

00:11:08.634 --> 00:11:11.904
Because all the energy doesn't
end up in the hands of a few.

00:11:11.904 --> 00:11:15.041 align:start size:94% position:6%
So you try to build
nuclear reactors perhaps,

00:11:15.041 --> 00:11:17.243
there's a bit of a problem
getting rid of the waste

00:11:17.243 --> 00:11:19.412 align:start size:84% position:16%
and there's a lot
of bureaucratic
wrangling around that.

00:11:19.412 --> 00:11:20.746
It's not a solved
problem entirely,

00:11:20.746 --> 00:11:22.348
but we don't have
a better option

00:11:22.348 --> 00:11:24.150
than nuclear in the long run,

00:11:24.150 --> 00:11:26.318
and so you make power
as cheap as possible.

00:11:26.318 --> 00:11:28.454
So if you wanna
save the climate,

00:11:28.454 --> 00:11:29.855
you wanna make power
everywhere in the world

00:11:29.855 --> 00:11:31.891
as cheap as possible,
as fast as possible

00:11:31.891 --> 00:11:33.659
to make people as
rich as possible

00:11:33.659 --> 00:11:34.860
because the richer they get,

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:36.228
the more they care
about the environment

00:11:36.228 --> 00:11:38.297 align:start size:88% position:13%
and that pattern is
clear in China and India

00:11:38.297 --> 00:11:41.567 align:start position:0%
which have greened substantially
in the last 20 years.

00:11:41.567 --> 00:11:43.402 line:0%
Those two countries
have greened in area

00:11:43.402 --> 00:11:45.871 line:0%
the size of the Amazon
in the last 20 years,

00:11:45.871 --> 00:11:48.441 line:0%
and that's an addition by the
way, just so everyone knows.

00:11:48.441 --> 00:11:50.342
Here's a cool little fact,

00:11:50.342 --> 00:11:53.312
you know that as carbon
dioxide levels rise,

00:11:53.312 --> 00:11:57.483 align:start position:0%
plants can grow in drier
environment, drier environments,

00:11:57.483 --> 00:11:59.018
semi-arid like deserts,

00:11:59.018 --> 00:12:03.689
like the sand banks
on oceans and lakes,

00:12:03.689 --> 00:12:05.424
stabilizing that as well.

00:12:05.424 --> 00:12:08.494
And so because the carbon
dioxide levels have risen,

00:12:08.494 --> 00:12:11.530
the plants can close their
pores, the breathing pores

00:12:11.530 --> 00:12:13.599 align:start size:88% position:13%
and so they don't
evaporate water as much,

00:12:13.599 --> 00:12:16.202
and so an area the size
of Alaska has greened

00:12:16.202 --> 00:12:18.037
in the last couple of decades

00:12:18.037 --> 00:12:21.107
in addition to what China
and India have planted.

00:12:22.007 --> 00:12:23.075
And so...

00:12:24.577 --> 00:12:27.379 align:start size:97% position:3%
I worked for the UN
Secretary General's Committee

00:12:27.379 --> 00:12:30.683
on Sustainable Development
and to some degree COP26

00:12:30.683 --> 00:12:33.119 align:start size:84% position:16%
is an attempt to bring
the corporate world

00:12:33.119 --> 00:12:35.421
in line with those
developmental goals.

00:12:35.421 --> 00:12:37.156
And there was a bunch of
problems with the project

00:12:37.156 --> 00:12:39.125 align:start size:94% position:6%
'cause there were 200
goals and that's too many.

00:12:39.125 --> 00:12:40.359
You have to prioritize them

00:12:40.359 --> 00:12:42.661
and no one was willing
to accept beyond Lomborg

00:12:42.661 --> 00:12:44.697 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that's a different
conversation.

00:12:44.697 --> 00:12:46.031
But what I learned,

00:12:46.031 --> 00:12:48.167 align:start size:84% position:16%
'cause I read about
200 books at that time

00:12:48.167 --> 00:12:50.503
on the environment and
sustainable development

00:12:50.503 --> 00:12:52.838
and what I learned was
things are way better

00:12:52.838 --> 00:12:54.373
than anybody thinks.

00:12:54.373 --> 00:12:56.876
We have a glorious future in
front of us if we want to.

00:12:56.876 --> 00:12:59.578 align:start position:0%
The best way to save the planet
is to make poor people rich

00:12:59.578 --> 00:13:01.347
as fast as we possibly can.

00:13:01.347 --> 00:13:03.182
So that means we can have
our cake and eat it too,

00:13:03.182 --> 00:13:05.718 align:start size:81% position:19%
and isn't that
absolutely lovely?

00:13:05.718 --> 00:13:08.354 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the biggest environmental
issue that's facing us,

00:13:08.354 --> 00:13:10.589
isn't global warming, it's
overfishing in the oceans,

00:13:10.589 --> 00:13:11.824
as far as I'm concerned

00:13:11.824 --> 00:13:13.592
because that's that we
could actually stop.

00:13:13.592 --> 00:13:16.495 align:start position:0%
It's actually extremely damaging
and we know how to stop it.

00:13:16.495 --> 00:13:18.931
And Trudeau to his
credit, by the way,

00:13:18.931 --> 00:13:23.936
has produced a series of
marine protected areas

00:13:25.371 --> 00:13:26.806
along Canada's coastline
that weren't there before.

00:13:26.806 --> 00:13:28.607
Now, I don't know how
protected they are

00:13:28.607 --> 00:13:30.142 align:start size:84% position:16%
and how thorough
that legislation is,

00:13:30.142 --> 00:13:33.746 align:start size:97% position:3%
but that's something that
he's done that actually counts

00:13:33.746 --> 00:13:37.249 align:start size:97% position:3%
as a credible accomplishment
in my estimation.

00:13:37.249 --> 00:13:38.083
- So-

00:13:38.083 --> 00:13:38.918
- But...

00:13:38.918 --> 00:13:40.519
Go ahead, Dave.

00:13:40.519 --> 00:13:42.655 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, first off,
I've interviewed Bjorn
a couple of times

00:13:42.655 --> 00:13:45.491 align:start size:84% position:16%
but his last book
basically illustrates,

00:13:45.491 --> 00:13:47.259 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, it's mostly
illustrations

00:13:47.259 --> 00:13:48.928 align:start size:97% position:3%
that lay out almost
everything you're saying here.

00:13:48.928 --> 00:13:50.696 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I just want people to
know you're not making it up.

00:13:50.696 --> 00:13:52.164
We'll link to that interview

00:13:52.164 --> 00:13:53.599 align:start size:88% position:13%
so they can see it, but-
- No, no.

00:13:53.599 --> 00:13:54.900
- yeah, go ahead.

00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:56.302
- Okay, so well look,

00:13:56.302 --> 00:13:59.505 align:start position:0%
so there's these 200 development
goals and sustainable,

00:13:59.505 --> 00:14:01.240 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's like, okay, well what
does sustainable mean?

00:14:01.240 --> 00:14:02.675
And over what timeframe?

00:14:02.675 --> 00:14:04.777 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's actually incredibly
complicated, right?

00:14:04.777 --> 00:14:07.012
Because timeframe
is a real problem.

00:14:07.012 --> 00:14:09.548 align:start size:84% position:16%
Do we mean sustainable
over 5,000 years?

00:14:09.548 --> 00:14:11.050
Well, why should we care?

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:12.418
Because God only knows what
the world is gonna be like

00:14:12.418 --> 00:14:13.853
in 5,000 years.

00:14:13.853 --> 00:14:15.788 align:start size:94% position:6%
Just look at what computer
technology is doing,

00:14:15.788 --> 00:14:17.089
we can't plan out that far.

00:14:17.089 --> 00:14:18.791
So that's probably too long.

00:14:18.791 --> 00:14:20.226
Hundred years?

00:14:20.226 --> 00:14:22.294
Well, we're all dead then.

00:14:22.294 --> 00:14:23.996
And you might think,
well, we should care.

00:14:23.996 --> 00:14:26.465
It's like, yeah, perhaps,
but how should we care?

00:14:26.465 --> 00:14:27.800 align:start size:84% position:16%
'Cause we can't
predict that far out.

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:30.302
So the timeframe
is really crucial.

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:34.306
And then the fact that
there are 200 goals,

00:14:34.306 --> 00:14:36.275
that's like zero goals.

00:14:36.275 --> 00:14:39.511
Like if you were trying to
do 200 things, first of all,

00:14:39.511 --> 00:14:41.180 align:start size:97% position:3%
at any moment, you wouldn't
be able to do anything

00:14:41.180 --> 00:14:43.916 align:start size:94% position:6%
because at any moment
you can only do one thing

00:14:43.916 --> 00:14:45.451
and so you have to prioritize.

00:14:45.451 --> 00:14:49.321
And I asked repeatedly when I
was working on this committee,

00:14:49.321 --> 00:14:52.391
why aren't these
goals prioritized?

00:14:52.391 --> 00:14:53.592
And the answer was,

00:14:53.592 --> 00:14:55.027
"Well, they all have
separate constituencies

00:14:55.027 --> 00:14:56.996
who are fighting for
that particular value

00:14:56.996 --> 00:15:00.432 align:start position:0%
and they get annoyed if there's
any sequential approach."

00:15:00.432 --> 00:15:02.668
It's like, okay, well, that
just means we do nothing

00:15:02.668 --> 00:15:05.571
because 200 goals is just chaos.

00:15:05.571 --> 00:15:07.773
And then I found Lomborg.

00:15:07.773 --> 00:15:09.174
And so what Lomborg does is,

00:15:09.174 --> 00:15:12.878 align:start size:97% position:3%
he hires teams of economists
to assess the goals

00:15:12.878 --> 00:15:15.714 align:start size:88% position:13%
in a given country or
globally for that matter

00:15:15.714 --> 00:15:17.416
by cost benefit.

00:15:17.416 --> 00:15:18.918
And you can argue about that

00:15:18.918 --> 00:15:21.053 align:start size:97% position:3%
because there's assumptions
that you have to use

00:15:21.053 --> 00:15:23.322
that are built into
your analysis, right?

00:15:23.322 --> 00:15:25.524
There's always priority axioms

00:15:25.524 --> 00:15:29.328 align:start size:97% position:3%
and prejudices and biases
and so forth and constraints,

00:15:29.328 --> 00:15:32.598 align:start size:97% position:3%
but he does it with 10
different teams of economists.

00:15:32.598 --> 00:15:34.066
So that's kind of good,

00:15:34.066 --> 00:15:36.435
and then he averages across the
whole set of the economists.

00:15:36.435 --> 00:15:37.369
And then you might say,

00:15:37.369 --> 00:15:38.904
"Well, it's an economic analysis

00:15:38.904 --> 00:15:41.206 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that doesn't take
everything into account."

00:15:41.206 --> 00:15:44.777 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's like a, everything
can't be taken into account.

00:15:44.777 --> 00:15:48.314 align:start size:81% position:19%
And b, do you have
a better idea?

00:15:48.314 --> 00:15:51.016 align:start size:97% position:3%
And if not, then, "Well,
what good is your criticism?"

00:15:52.418 --> 00:15:54.653 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like, I, haven't seen a better
idea than Lomborg's idea,

00:15:54.653 --> 00:15:55.854
but from anyone.

00:15:58.624 --> 00:16:01.293 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Is part of the
problem that people,

00:16:01.293 --> 00:16:04.897 align:start size:97% position:3%
because our institutions have
in so many ways failed us

00:16:04.897 --> 00:16:06.565 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I know you've
obviously talked about it

00:16:06.565 --> 00:16:08.033
and wrote about it
in your last book

00:16:08.033 --> 00:16:09.768 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we shouldn't just
needlessly wanna burn down

00:16:09.768 --> 00:16:11.003
these institutions.

00:16:11.003 --> 00:16:13.205 align:start size:84% position:16%
But they've sort of
failed us to the point

00:16:13.205 --> 00:16:15.040
that when they do
these conferences,

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:16.508
like these climate conferences,

00:16:16.508 --> 00:16:19.078 align:start size:88% position:13%
I was listening to
Obama's speech yesterday

00:16:19.078 --> 00:16:20.980
and he's going on and on about

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:22.948
how we're gonna have to
sacrifice and all that.

00:16:22.948 --> 00:16:24.183
Meanwhile, he just spoke of-

00:16:24.183 --> 00:16:25.718
- Yeah, who is gonna
have to sacrifice?

00:16:25.718 --> 00:16:27.486
Who is gonna have to sacrifice?

00:16:27.486 --> 00:16:29.488 align:start size:84% position:16%
And exactly what?
- $15 million house.

00:16:29.488 --> 00:16:31.757 align:start size:94% position:6%
He just bought a $15
million house on the water

00:16:31.757 --> 00:16:32.992
in Martha's vineyard.

00:16:32.992 --> 00:16:34.693
I was just down in
Miami, trust me,

00:16:34.693 --> 00:16:35.627 align:start size:84% position:16%
there's plenty of
$40 million mansions.

00:16:35.627 --> 00:16:36.829
- Well, that's a sacrifice.

00:16:36.829 --> 00:16:37.896
Well, there you go, Dave.

00:16:37.896 --> 00:16:39.164
That's a sacrifice

00:16:39.164 --> 00:16:41.066
because he didn't buy
a $40 million mansion.

00:16:41.066 --> 00:16:42.468
(laughs)

00:16:42.468 --> 00:16:44.570 align:start position:0%
- Right, so we should be
thankful for him as he flew in.

00:16:44.570 --> 00:16:45.971 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, so, let's
talk about sacrifice.

00:16:45.971 --> 00:16:46.805 align:start size:84% position:16%
- But yeah, what
about that break down?

00:16:46.805 --> 00:16:48.340
- Yeah, okay.

00:16:48.340 --> 00:16:50.376
So yeah, let's let the old
people freeze in the dark.

00:16:51.877 --> 00:16:53.145
That's what's gonna
happen in Europe

00:16:53.145 --> 00:16:54.380
if we get a cold winter.

00:16:55.547 --> 00:16:57.316
So who's gonna sacrifice here?

00:16:57.316 --> 00:16:58.550
Well, I can tell you
the answer to that

00:16:58.550 --> 00:17:01.153
and let's play leftist
here for a minute, okay?

00:17:01.153 --> 00:17:03.389
So there are hierarchies,

00:17:04.623 --> 00:17:05.858 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I think they're
competence hierarchies

00:17:05.858 --> 00:17:08.360 align:start size:88% position:13%
but they're corrupted
by power to some degree.

00:17:08.360 --> 00:17:10.829 align:start size:84% position:16%
And how much is worthy
of investigation

00:17:10.829 --> 00:17:12.364 align:start size:84% position:16%
and we should always
clean them up.

00:17:12.364 --> 00:17:14.166
I mean, you just did
that in some sense

00:17:14.166 --> 00:17:16.268 align:start size:97% position:3%
when you said our institutions
are corrupt, right?

00:17:16.268 --> 00:17:19.271 align:start size:97% position:3%
And we have to be very
judicious, which institutions?

00:17:19.271 --> 00:17:20.072
Where are they corrupt?

00:17:20.072 --> 00:17:21.306
How are they corrupt?

00:17:21.306 --> 00:17:24.376 align:start size:88% position:13%
Add a level of detail
and analyzed in a manner

00:17:24.376 --> 00:17:25.577
that allows us to fix them

00:17:25.577 --> 00:17:27.413 align:start size:84% position:16%
instead of replace
them wholesale, right?

00:17:27.413 --> 00:17:28.814
So, okay.

00:17:28.814 --> 00:17:33.585 align:start size:97% position:3%
In any case, in a hierarchy,
when there's stress,

00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:36.388 align:start size:88% position:13%
illness and death climb
from the bottom up.

00:17:37.823 --> 00:17:39.925 align:start size:97% position:3%
That's partly why you wanna
be near the top in a hierarchy

00:17:39.925 --> 00:17:44.930 align:start size:97% position:3%
'cause it's actually
psychophysiological easier.

00:17:46.498 --> 00:17:49.201 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well, obviously
'cause otherwise, why
would you strive for?

00:17:49.201 --> 00:17:50.636
There are other reasons,

00:17:50.636 --> 00:17:52.805
maybe you want to do do
good for other people,

00:17:52.805 --> 00:17:54.039
maybe you're motivated by power,

00:17:54.039 --> 00:17:55.274
maybe you're a psychopath,

00:17:55.274 --> 00:17:56.975 align:start size:84% position:16%
maybe you're acting
instrumentally,

00:17:56.975 --> 00:17:58.077
there's lots of other reasons.

00:17:58.077 --> 00:17:59.845 align:start size:88% position:13%
But one of the
consequences of success

00:17:59.845 --> 00:18:01.947
is that you don't die.

00:18:02.948 --> 00:18:04.216
So who dies first?

00:18:04.216 --> 00:18:05.818
Well, as soon as you
stress the system,

00:18:05.818 --> 00:18:08.153 align:start size:84% position:16%
let's say we double
energy prices.

00:18:08.153 --> 00:18:09.288
Well, who's gonna sit...

00:18:09.288 --> 00:18:11.023
What's the sacrifice?

00:18:11.023 --> 00:18:13.492
Well, old poor people

00:18:13.492 --> 00:18:15.427 align:start size:88% position:13%
'cause they're more
susceptible to see death

00:18:15.427 --> 00:18:17.463 align:start size:84% position:16%
when they can't afford
air conditioning,

00:18:17.463 --> 00:18:19.498
when they can't afford heat.

00:18:19.498 --> 00:18:20.933
And so, yeah,

00:18:20.933 --> 00:18:22.301 align:start size:97% position:3%
but there's too many people
on the planet anyway.

00:18:22.301 --> 00:18:27.206
So what the hell we
kill a few, whatever.

00:18:27.206 --> 00:18:28.474
They're just poor people.

00:18:28.474 --> 00:18:30.008
And this is the thing
that bugs me so much.

00:18:30.008 --> 00:18:34.680
It's like, "Okay, leftists,
which side are you on here?

00:18:36.048 --> 00:18:39.451 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's like, "Are you
gonna, sacrifice the poor

00:18:39.451 --> 00:18:41.353 align:start size:84% position:16%
to your hypothetical
future utopia?"

00:18:41.353 --> 00:18:43.055 align:start size:84% position:16%
"Yeah, but they're
all gonna die anyways,

00:18:43.055 --> 00:18:44.623 align:start size:81% position:19%
because the planet
is gonna burn."

00:18:44.623 --> 00:18:45.958
No, it's not.

00:18:45.958 --> 00:18:47.993
The IPCC never said that.

00:18:49.328 --> 00:18:53.132
So Bjorn has modeled
out a reasonable amount

00:18:53.132 --> 00:18:57.102 align:start size:97% position:3%
of climate change in
meaning temperature increase

00:18:57.102 --> 00:18:58.604
over the next hundred years,

00:18:58.604 --> 00:19:03.242
he's taken what's promoted
as the scientific consensus.

00:19:04.276 --> 00:19:05.210 align:start size:84% position:16%
There is no
consensus in science.

00:19:05.210 --> 00:19:06.712
That's not how science works.

00:19:06.712 --> 00:19:08.280
It's not a consensus enterprise.

00:19:08.280 --> 00:19:10.349
But nonetheless, he's
playing along with it.

00:19:10.349 --> 00:19:12.951
Okay, we'll take your figures.

00:19:12.951 --> 00:19:15.254 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's gonna produce
somewhat of a detriment

00:19:17.322 --> 00:19:22.294
in global economic growth but
growth is about 4% a year.

00:19:22.294 --> 00:19:24.796
And so even if that
declines a chunk

00:19:24.796 --> 00:19:26.465
because of global warming,

00:19:26.465 --> 00:19:29.268
everyone's still gonna be
like five times as rich

00:19:29.268 --> 00:19:30.669 align:start size:81% position:19%
as they are now
in a hundred years

00:19:30.669 --> 00:19:31.937
if we don't screw things up

00:19:31.937 --> 00:19:34.673
and so maybe they'll
be four times as rich.

00:19:34.673 --> 00:19:37.376
Well, that's a cost
and fair enough,

00:19:37.376 --> 00:19:39.144
and if we can ameliorate it,

00:19:39.144 --> 00:19:43.382
we should probably try not to
change natural environments

00:19:43.382 --> 00:19:45.651 align:start size:81% position:19%
any faster than
we have to, right?

00:19:45.651 --> 00:19:48.453 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because of unexpected
consequences.

00:19:48.453 --> 00:19:50.122
It's a reasonable argument.

00:19:50.122 --> 00:19:54.526
But here's another
thing, this net zero.

00:19:54.526 --> 00:19:56.695 align:start position:0%
Okay, as soon as you hear
someone say anything like that,

00:19:56.695 --> 00:19:59.431 align:start size:84% position:16%
zero, you know,
they're not thinking.

00:19:59.431 --> 00:20:00.666 align:start size:84% position:16%
- It's like zero
tolerance for drugs.

00:20:00.666 --> 00:20:02.201
- You mean we can't get
COVID deaths to zero?

00:20:02.201 --> 00:20:03.001
We shouldn't be trying to
get COVID deaths to zero?

00:20:03.001 --> 00:20:04.069
- Yeah, right, exactly.

00:20:04.069 --> 00:20:05.571
Exactly.

00:20:05.571 --> 00:20:07.873
Well, and we don't have
zero tolerance for drugs.

00:20:07.873 --> 00:20:09.575
Zero is the wrong number.

00:20:09.575 --> 00:20:10.809
Zero pollution.

00:20:12.110 --> 00:20:13.679
Well, no.

00:20:13.679 --> 00:20:16.782 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, cows, there's
manure from cows,

00:20:17.649 --> 00:20:19.017
people go to the bathroom.

00:20:20.385 --> 00:20:22.020
There's not zero pollution.

00:20:23.222 --> 00:20:26.725
So net zero, it's an
empty talking point

00:20:28.126 --> 00:20:29.528 align:start size:94% position:6%
to put up a flag that
you're on the right side.

00:20:29.528 --> 00:20:32.931 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's not differentiated,
careful, thoughtful,

00:20:32.931 --> 00:20:35.534
thorough detail
oriented thinking

00:20:35.534 --> 00:20:38.270 align:start size:97% position:3%
at the level that will
actually produce solutions.

00:20:38.270 --> 00:20:39.771 align:start size:84% position:16%
And there's no
discussion about like,

00:20:39.771 --> 00:20:41.273
what are we aiming at here?

00:20:41.273 --> 00:20:45.010 align:start size:88% position:13%
Well, how about copious
energy for everyone

00:20:45.010 --> 00:20:46.612
as clean as possible?

00:20:46.612 --> 00:20:47.980
But in that order.

00:20:47.980 --> 00:20:50.148
Copious energy for everyone,

00:20:50.148 --> 00:20:51.883
lowest possible price,

00:20:51.883 --> 00:20:53.418
resilient stable systems.

00:20:53.418 --> 00:20:56.188 align:start size:84% position:16%
And to their credit,
the Democrats

00:20:56.188 --> 00:20:59.524
and their Infrastructure Bill
are building resilience goals

00:20:59.524 --> 00:21:02.594 align:start size:97% position:3%
in relationship to the power
grid and that's necessary

00:21:02.594 --> 00:21:03.996
because we should
be stress testing

00:21:03.996 --> 00:21:05.564
those systems constantly.

00:21:05.564 --> 00:21:08.333
But if you're on the left and
you care about poor people,

00:21:08.333 --> 00:21:11.003 align:start position:0%
the only thing you should really
care about is cheap energy.

00:21:11.003 --> 00:21:12.204
And why is that?

00:21:12.204 --> 00:21:14.806 align:start size:84% position:16%
Let's be very precise
about this, okay?

00:21:14.806 --> 00:21:19.177 align:start size:84% position:16%
What makes people
prosperous and secure?

00:21:19.177 --> 00:21:20.012
Work.

00:21:21.046 --> 00:21:22.047
What is work?

00:21:22.047 --> 00:21:25.450
It is the expenditure of energy.

00:21:25.450 --> 00:21:26.585
What is energy?

00:21:26.585 --> 00:21:27.419
Work.

00:21:27.419 --> 00:21:28.387
What is work?

00:21:28.387 --> 00:21:29.588
Wealth.

00:21:29.588 --> 00:21:31.423
You wanna deal with
absolute poverty

00:21:31.423 --> 00:21:33.759 align:start size:88% position:13%
or even relative
poverty for that matter,

00:21:33.759 --> 00:21:35.994
energy is everything.

00:21:35.994 --> 00:21:38.297
And here's another fact.

00:21:38.297 --> 00:21:41.033
So you Americans have actually

00:21:41.033 --> 00:21:43.602
knocked your carbon
dioxide output down

00:21:43.602 --> 00:21:46.405 align:start size:81% position:19%
14% in the last
couple of decades.

00:21:46.405 --> 00:21:47.306
You know why?

00:21:48.707 --> 00:21:49.608
Fracking.

00:21:50.842 --> 00:21:53.378
'Cause it made natural gas cheap

00:21:53.378 --> 00:21:55.180 align:start size:84% position:16%
and replaced dirtier
forms of energy.

00:21:55.180 --> 00:22:00.152 align:start size:84% position:16%
Plus it made you way
less reliant on OPEC
and the Russians.

00:22:01.353 --> 00:22:03.455
And how is that not good
for planetary stability?

00:22:03.455 --> 00:22:05.891
You talk about the environment,

00:22:05.891 --> 00:22:08.193
how about not a third world war?

00:22:09.695 --> 00:22:12.798 align:start size:94% position:6%
'Cause that's not so
good for the environment.

00:22:12.798 --> 00:22:15.600 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so fracking knocked carbon
dioxide output in the U.S.

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:17.235
down 14%.

00:22:17.235 --> 00:22:19.604
Who would have predicted that?

00:22:19.604 --> 00:22:22.441
And in Shellenberger's
book, "Apocalypse Never,"

00:22:23.642 --> 00:22:25.510
he interviewed an MIT
scientist and he said,

00:22:25.510 --> 00:22:28.080 align:start size:97% position:3%
you really wanna knock
carbon dioxide output down,

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:30.649
make India and China,

00:22:30.649 --> 00:22:32.851 align:start size:94% position:6%
but India, he was speaking
of more particularly,

00:22:32.851 --> 00:22:35.053
switch to coal as
fast as possible.

00:22:35.053 --> 00:22:36.488
You think, coal?

00:22:36.488 --> 00:22:37.422
Why?

00:22:37.422 --> 00:22:39.491
Oh, it's better than wood.

00:22:39.491 --> 00:22:43.261
And we know the pattern,
wood, that's not so good.

00:22:43.261 --> 00:22:45.163
You'd cut down trees,
you cut down forest,

00:22:45.163 --> 00:22:47.232 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's really polluting,
especially indoors

00:22:47.232 --> 00:22:48.567 line:0%
and that kills a
lot of people's,

00:22:48.567 --> 00:22:50.569 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
stunts a lot of
children's growth,

00:22:50.569 --> 00:22:52.738 line:0%
growth in the broad sense

00:22:52.738 --> 00:22:54.439 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
'cause indoor pollution
is much more deadly

00:22:54.439 --> 00:22:56.641 align:start size:88% position:13%
than outdoor pollution,
generally speaking.

00:22:56.641 --> 00:22:59.277
So, and wood, well, you
have to cut down forests.

00:22:59.277 --> 00:23:00.545
It's inefficient,

00:23:00.545 --> 00:23:01.847
it puts particulates in
the atmosphere as well.

00:23:01.847 --> 00:23:03.415
So you switch to coal.

00:23:03.415 --> 00:23:04.616
Coal is better.

00:23:04.616 --> 00:23:06.685
It's not wood, to begin with.

00:23:06.685 --> 00:23:08.353
And then it's not
near as polluting

00:23:08.353 --> 00:23:10.422
and coal technologies
have got quite clean.

00:23:10.422 --> 00:23:11.757
Now you still have
to mine the coal

00:23:11.757 --> 00:23:13.225 align:start size:97% position:3%
and there's an
environmental cost for that.

00:23:13.225 --> 00:23:15.394 align:start size:94% position:6%
Well, then you switch
from coal to natural gas.

00:23:15.394 --> 00:23:17.195
Natural gas is pretty damn good.

00:23:17.195 --> 00:23:19.831
There's lots of it, it's
plentiful, it's clean.

00:23:19.831 --> 00:23:22.768 align:start size:88% position:13%
And then maybe, if we
can get our act together

00:23:22.768 --> 00:23:24.002
and have some sense,

00:23:24.002 --> 00:23:27.172
we could switch from
natural gas to fusion.

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:30.475
Well, and then we could
have energy for everyone

00:23:30.475 --> 00:23:31.810
at the lowest possible cost.

00:23:31.810 --> 00:23:32.644
And then what happens?

00:23:32.644 --> 00:23:33.478
Well, people get rich.

00:23:33.478 --> 00:23:34.413
And then what happens?

00:23:34.413 --> 00:23:35.680
They care about the environment.

00:23:35.680 --> 00:23:39.151 align:start size:84% position:16%
So how is that not
the right solution to,

00:23:39.151 --> 00:23:43.255 align:start position:0%
let's not call it climate
change because that's annoying.

00:23:43.255 --> 00:23:46.758
It's like, let's not produce
any more waste than we have to

00:23:46.758 --> 00:23:49.194
while we try to live.

00:23:49.194 --> 00:23:50.629
Fair enough, you know.

00:23:50.629 --> 00:23:52.230
That's a reasonable goal.

00:23:52.230 --> 00:23:55.901
But all I see coming out
of these COP26 conferences,

00:23:55.901 --> 00:23:57.436
that sort of thing is,

00:23:57.436 --> 00:24:00.172 align:start size:97% position:3%
here's a bunch of people doing
it wrong and let stop them.

00:24:00.172 --> 00:24:02.007
It's like, yeah,
yeah, right guys.

00:24:02.007 --> 00:24:04.476
Why don't you try to
do something instead?

00:24:04.476 --> 00:24:06.411 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Okay, that's exactly where
I wanted to go with this.

00:24:06.411 --> 00:24:09.114 align:start size:88% position:13%
Are we just at the point
where the machinery,

00:24:09.114 --> 00:24:11.283
the institutions,
the media tech,

00:24:11.283 --> 00:24:14.686
the whole machinery of how
we communicate to each other

00:24:14.686 --> 00:24:16.288
has broken down
to the point that

00:24:16.288 --> 00:24:17.956
if everything you
said right there,

00:24:17.956 --> 00:24:20.859
you stepped into an office
and you were sitting down

00:24:20.859 --> 00:24:23.161 align:start size:88% position:13%
with all of those
people, or even just AOC

00:24:23.161 --> 00:24:25.497
and the people here that
want The Green New Deal,

00:24:25.497 --> 00:24:27.199
and you laid all that out that

00:24:27.199 --> 00:24:30.202 align:start size:97% position:3%
they will basically just say-
- No, I don't think.

00:24:30.202 --> 00:24:31.703
- "Well, okay, good for him.

00:24:31.703 --> 00:24:32.838
He's got some studies."

00:24:32.838 --> 00:24:34.039
- No, I don't believe that.

00:24:34.039 --> 00:24:35.273 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't believe that.
- Yeah?

00:24:35.273 --> 00:24:36.775
- I think we have
to be judicious

00:24:36.775 --> 00:24:38.810
in our institutional criticisms.

00:24:38.810 --> 00:24:40.445
Well, there's a bunch
of reasons for that.

00:24:40.445 --> 00:24:44.716
First of all, that
infrastructure bill
that was just passed,

00:24:44.716 --> 00:24:46.885 align:start size:97% position:3%
that's nowhere near as
stupid as it could have been.

00:24:46.885 --> 00:24:49.321
And if you look at the
White House messaging,

00:24:49.321 --> 00:24:50.522 align:start size:84% position:16%
well, that's
something, isn't it?

00:24:50.522 --> 00:24:51.857
- Yeah, it's something.

00:24:51.857 --> 00:24:53.091 align:start size:84% position:16%
- It's something, man.
- Yeah.

00:24:53.091 --> 00:24:54.559
- And if you look at the
White House messaging,

00:24:54.559 --> 00:24:58.463
it's about 2% diversity,
inclusivity and equity

00:24:58.463 --> 00:25:01.633
and that's not too bad 'cause
zero is asking for too much,

00:25:01.633 --> 00:25:03.168
as we already discussed.

00:25:03.168 --> 00:25:05.904
And I've talked to
lots of Democrats

00:25:05.904 --> 00:25:09.407
and lots of Republicans and
there are people of goodwill

00:25:09.407 --> 00:25:12.344
who are competent on both
sides and they're trying.

00:25:12.344 --> 00:25:14.412
Well, people don't understand.

00:25:14.412 --> 00:25:16.248
So your congressmen federally.

00:25:17.782 --> 00:25:19.417 align:start size:81% position:19%
All right, so I
went to Washington

00:25:19.417 --> 00:25:21.786
and I talked to a whole bunch
of them trying to find out.

00:25:21.786 --> 00:25:24.256
I read this book a long time
ago and called, "Systemantics."

00:25:24.256 --> 00:25:26.424
It's a great book by John Gall.

00:25:26.424 --> 00:25:28.493 align:start size:81% position:19%
It's a great book,
"Systemantics."

00:25:29.528 --> 00:25:31.196
One of its dictums is,

00:25:31.196 --> 00:25:34.966 align:start size:97% position:3%
the system does not do
what its name says it does.

00:25:34.966 --> 00:25:36.635 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so then the question
is, what does it do?

00:25:36.635 --> 00:25:38.236
Well, then you have to look.

00:25:38.236 --> 00:25:40.906 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I worked at Social Services
for the Alberta government

00:25:40.906 --> 00:25:42.107
when I was a kid.

00:25:42.107 --> 00:25:45.277 align:start size:84% position:16%
And social services,
what do they do?

00:25:45.277 --> 00:25:48.179
Well, I was tasked weirdly
enough when I was about 19

00:25:48.179 --> 00:25:52.651
to find out, to update a
big consulting company's

00:25:52.651 --> 00:25:56.121
analysis of spending of the
Social Services Department.

00:25:56.121 --> 00:25:58.657 align:start position:0%
They had paid them some
hundreds of thousands of dollars

00:25:58.657 --> 00:26:00.158 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I was just working
as a summer intern

00:26:00.158 --> 00:26:01.560
and they said, "Update this."

00:26:01.560 --> 00:26:03.828
And I thought, "Huh, okay."

00:26:03.828 --> 00:26:05.730
And so I phoned around
trying to get the figures

00:26:05.730 --> 00:26:07.198
and no one knew what they were.

00:26:07.198 --> 00:26:10.669
The system had no idea where
the money it was spending went.

00:26:10.669 --> 00:26:12.904 align:start size:81% position:19%
So I was trying
to get stats like,

00:26:12.904 --> 00:26:14.973 align:start size:84% position:16%
how much of the
welfare money spent

00:26:14.973 --> 00:26:17.275
actually goes to recipients?

00:26:17.275 --> 00:26:18.910
Couldn't find the data.

00:26:18.910 --> 00:26:20.812
And then I realized, okay,

00:26:20.812 --> 00:26:23.148
social services,
what does it do?

00:26:23.148 --> 00:26:26.351
Probably spends 95% of
its money on bureaucrats

00:26:26.351 --> 00:26:28.553 align:start size:88% position:13%
who are implementing
social service programs.

00:26:28.553 --> 00:26:29.788
And then I thought,

00:26:29.788 --> 00:26:31.323
"Well, should I be
cynical about that?"

00:26:31.323 --> 00:26:33.391
It's like, well, no.

00:26:33.391 --> 00:26:34.993
It's actually hard
to distribute money,

00:26:34.993 --> 00:26:37.195 align:start size:84% position:16%
and if 5% of it
goes to its target,

00:26:37.195 --> 00:26:38.563
that might not be too bad.

00:26:38.563 --> 00:26:39.998
It's not great by the way

00:26:39.998 --> 00:26:43.602 align:start size:97% position:3%
but it's better than running
at a deficit or zero.

00:26:43.602 --> 00:26:46.204 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so many charities
are like that.

00:26:46.204 --> 00:26:48.006 align:start size:81% position:19%
If they get 10%
to the recipients,

00:26:48.006 --> 00:26:49.174
they're doing pretty well.

00:26:49.174 --> 00:26:50.875 align:start size:84% position:16%
And you can be
cynical about that.

00:26:52.310 --> 00:26:54.379
So in any case, when I went
to Washington, I thought,

00:26:54.379 --> 00:26:56.848
"Okay, these are
Congress people.

00:26:56.848 --> 00:26:57.949
What do they do?"

00:26:59.784 --> 00:27:01.753
Well, hypothetically,
they analyze policy,

00:27:01.753 --> 00:27:04.522
they set out strategy, they
talk to their constituents.

00:27:04.522 --> 00:27:07.258 align:start size:88% position:13%
No, they spend 25 hours
a week fundraising.

00:27:07.258 --> 00:27:10.295
25 hours a week, both sides.

00:27:11.696 --> 00:27:13.164 align:start size:94% position:6%
They can't do it in their
congressional offices

00:27:13.164 --> 00:27:14.666
'cause that's illegal.

00:27:14.666 --> 00:27:17.268
So they have other offices in
these industrial warehouses

00:27:17.268 --> 00:27:18.670
with drop ceilings

00:27:18.670 --> 00:27:21.873 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they basically are
telemarketers 25 hours a week.

00:27:21.873 --> 00:27:24.643 align:start size:97% position:3%
Well, how the hell can those
people get anything done?

00:27:24.643 --> 00:27:27.045 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then I had lunches where
there was some Democrats

00:27:27.045 --> 00:27:29.180 align:start size:97% position:3%
and some Republicans,
generally more junior members

00:27:29.180 --> 00:27:30.682
just trying to get
to know each other

00:27:30.682 --> 00:27:31.916
and I had them go
around the table

00:27:31.916 --> 00:27:33.618
and say why they went
into public service

00:27:33.618 --> 00:27:36.488 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you couldn't have
asked for more patriotic,

00:27:36.488 --> 00:27:39.658 align:start size:84% position:16%
heartfelt declarations
of virtuous intent.

00:27:39.658 --> 00:27:41.993 align:start position:0%
And you couldn't tell the
Democrats from the Republicans,

00:27:41.993 --> 00:27:44.262
not on the basis of that.

00:27:44.262 --> 00:27:45.664
And then you say,

00:27:45.664 --> 00:27:48.099 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Well, why did they
fundraise 25 hours a week?"

00:27:48.099 --> 00:27:50.802 align:start size:88% position:13%
Well they have to be
elected every two years?

00:27:50.802 --> 00:27:53.805
And then the party apparatus
because they don't really know

00:27:53.805 --> 00:27:56.041
how to spend money effectively,

00:27:57.676 --> 00:27:59.978
communicating with people to
get them to vote for them.

00:27:59.978 --> 00:28:01.713
They think money will do it.

00:28:01.713 --> 00:28:02.914
They won't support them

00:28:02.914 --> 00:28:04.949 align:start size:88% position:13%
unless they raise a
certain amount of money.

00:28:04.949 --> 00:28:07.919 align:start size:94% position:6%
Well, how the hell can
those people do their job?

00:28:07.919 --> 00:28:09.621 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so will you sit down
and you talk to them

00:28:09.621 --> 00:28:12.123
and the vast majority of them,

00:28:13.291 --> 00:28:15.293
they're even self-sacrificing.

00:28:15.293 --> 00:28:16.928
It's not a job I would want.

