00:47:13.597 --> 00:47:15.999
like say, "Well, how about
your third year seminars,

00:47:15.999 --> 00:47:17.868
fourth year seminars have

00:47:17.868 --> 00:47:20.838
30 people in them
instead of 15."

00:47:20.838 --> 00:47:23.841
Oh it's like, "Well then
they're not a seminar."

00:47:23.841 --> 00:47:26.210
So then I say, "Well
how about we just say,

00:47:26.210 --> 00:47:29.046
'No, hire more faculty.'"

00:47:29.046 --> 00:47:31.682
Just, "No, we will not do that."

00:47:31.682 --> 00:47:33.150
Well,

00:47:33.150 --> 00:47:35.819 align:start size:97% position:3%
they would've capitulated
instantly the administration,

00:47:35.819 --> 00:47:38.622
if we would've said no for sure.

00:47:38.622 --> 00:47:40.257
But they never said no.

00:47:40.257 --> 00:47:41.892 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I said, "Well,
why won't you say no?"

00:47:41.892 --> 00:47:44.061
"Well, they won't give
us what we want then."

00:47:44.061 --> 00:47:45.963 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Well, they don't give you
what you want anyways."

00:47:45.963 --> 00:47:48.298 align:start size:88% position:13%
I saw an endless number
of five year plans

00:47:48.298 --> 00:47:50.300 align:start size:94% position:6%
come and go where
nothing that was promised

00:47:50.300 --> 00:47:51.735
was ever delivered.

00:47:51.735 --> 00:47:53.170 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm not being
cynical about this,

00:47:53.170 --> 00:47:55.572 align:start position:0%
I mean the University of Toronto
ran pretty reasonably for

00:47:55.572 --> 00:47:58.976 align:start size:97% position:3%
a overgrown and heavily
bureaucratized institution.

00:48:00.344 --> 00:48:02.212 align:start size:94% position:6%
So it was all these
retreats while then these

00:48:02.212 --> 00:48:04.381
insane ideas came in.

00:48:04.381 --> 00:48:06.416
And here's the fundamental idea.

00:48:07.751 --> 00:48:09.052
And it really deals
with the mystery,

00:48:09.052 --> 00:48:10.354
I'm writing this new
book about it called

00:48:10.354 --> 00:48:12.456
"We Who Wrestle with God."

00:48:12.456 --> 00:48:13.824
We don't know how

00:48:13.824 --> 00:48:15.859
we manage the act of perception.

00:48:17.060 --> 00:48:19.563
We actually don't know,
it's really complicated.

00:48:19.563 --> 00:48:22.666
That's why we don't have
general per purpose robots,

00:48:22.666 --> 00:48:24.868 align:start size:94% position:6%
is because perceiving
the world turns out to be

00:48:24.868 --> 00:48:26.803
way more complicated than
anybody ever imagined.

00:48:26.803 --> 00:48:28.272
'Cause we kind of think,

00:48:28.272 --> 00:48:29.907
"Well, there's objects out
there and you just see them."

00:48:29.907 --> 00:48:32.609
It's like, if you take
a picture of a landscape

00:48:32.609 --> 00:48:34.711
and you put it in Photoshop
and then you blow it up,

00:48:34.711 --> 00:48:37.481 align:start size:97% position:3%
you can't tell where the
lines are between the objects.

00:48:37.481 --> 00:48:39.583
Well, the real world
is kind of like that.

00:48:40.717 --> 00:48:43.287 align:start size:84% position:16%
and even defining
simple things is like,

00:48:43.287 --> 00:48:45.455
What's a table?

00:48:45.455 --> 00:48:48.025 align:start size:88% position:13%
Well, it's a flat
surface with four legs.

00:48:48.025 --> 00:48:51.929 align:start size:94% position:6%
Well, okay, is a
dollhouse table, a table?

00:48:51.929 --> 00:48:53.430
How about if you put a

00:48:53.430 --> 00:48:55.933
plate full of food on a
stump and sit down to it,

00:48:55.933 --> 00:48:57.401
is that a table?

00:48:57.401 --> 00:49:00.504 align:start size:94% position:6%
What if the stump is
actually kind of slanted?

00:49:00.504 --> 00:49:01.838
What if it has a soft surface?

00:49:01.838 --> 00:49:03.941
Like maybe you put a
plate on a bean bag,

00:49:03.941 --> 00:49:05.842
is that now a table?

00:49:05.842 --> 00:49:07.878
Well, if a table might not be

00:49:07.878 --> 00:49:11.081 align:start size:97% position:3%
a flat surface with four
legs or one leg or three legs

00:49:11.081 --> 00:49:12.749
or a triangular surface,

00:49:12.749 --> 00:49:15.452 align:start size:97% position:3%
it might be a place you
put a plate full of food on

00:49:15.452 --> 00:49:18.655
to it down in eat at, which
is a functional definition.

00:49:18.655 --> 00:49:21.358 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it turns out that
most of the things we see

00:49:21.358 --> 00:49:25.128 align:start size:94% position:6%
are actually functional
icons rather than objects.

00:49:25.128 --> 00:49:27.197
And then you can't get
a machine to see that

00:49:27.197 --> 00:49:29.166
because they're not embodied.

00:49:29.166 --> 00:49:31.635 align:start size:84% position:16%
In any case, the
mystery of perception,

00:49:31.635 --> 00:49:32.936
how do we categorize?

00:49:32.936 --> 00:49:34.204
It's a great mystery,

00:49:34.204 --> 00:49:35.973
and the answer the left

00:49:35.973 --> 00:49:39.076
lept to in the face of
this immense mystery,

00:49:39.076 --> 00:49:42.379
which is how can finite
creatures comprehend the

00:49:42.379 --> 00:49:44.514 align:start size:84% position:16%
infinite well
enough to function?

00:49:44.514 --> 00:49:45.916
That's the mystery.

00:49:45.916 --> 00:49:47.284
Their answer was,

00:49:47.284 --> 00:49:48.618 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Well, all our categories
are based on the

00:49:48.618 --> 00:49:50.754
expression of will to power."

00:49:50.754 --> 00:49:53.857
And that's actually partly true,

00:49:55.225 --> 00:49:58.095 align:start size:97% position:3%
because part of our motivation
is move up hierarchies,

00:49:58.095 --> 00:49:59.896 align:start size:84% position:16%
but that can be
based on competence,

00:49:59.896 --> 00:50:02.399 align:start size:94% position:6%
and all of our
institutions are corrupted

00:50:02.399 --> 00:50:03.967
by power and deceit.

00:50:03.967 --> 00:50:05.235
So if they would've said,

00:50:06.536 --> 00:50:10.073 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Part of our categorization
is will to power."

00:50:10.073 --> 00:50:12.909
No problem, it would
left a mystery open.

00:50:12.909 --> 00:50:14.411
And this is a huge mystery,

00:50:14.411 --> 00:50:17.080 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's a religious mystery in
the final analysis, I believe.

00:50:17.080 --> 00:50:21.818 align:start position:0%
And if you have faith, you
believe that it's something like

00:50:21.818 --> 00:50:26.156 align:start size:97% position:3%
the spirit of goodness itself
that does categorization,

00:50:26.156 --> 00:50:28.392
that lays out our
basic perceptions.

00:50:28.392 --> 00:50:29.693
And that would depend
on your motivation,

00:50:29.693 --> 00:50:30.727
so we could say like,

00:50:30.727 --> 00:50:32.729
if you were oriented

00:50:32.729 --> 00:50:36.033
in the highest manner
you could be oriented,

00:50:36.033 --> 00:50:39.703 align:start size:88% position:13%
then the contents of
your categories would be

00:50:39.703 --> 00:50:41.938
subservient to the good.

00:50:41.938 --> 00:50:44.207
And I think you have
to be immensely cynical

00:50:44.207 --> 00:50:45.542
to think that will to power

00:50:45.542 --> 00:50:48.011
is a more powerful
force than that.

00:50:48.011 --> 00:50:50.747
And that sort of cynicism
that devours the world,

00:50:51.648 --> 00:50:53.917
and so we don't wanna do that.

00:50:53.917 --> 00:50:57.187 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I can see why the
universities fell into that

00:50:57.187 --> 00:51:00.557
because I think this was the
biggest mystery that emerged

00:51:00.557 --> 00:51:03.326
philosophically in the last
half of the 20th century.

00:51:03.326 --> 00:51:04.261
It's like, "Oh my."...

00:51:04.261 --> 00:51:05.395
See, 'cause what happened on.

00:51:05.395 --> 00:51:07.397 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the English
Departments it was...

00:51:07.397 --> 00:51:10.200
Well, we don't know
how we perceive even

00:51:10.200 --> 00:51:12.102
a simple landscape.

00:51:12.102 --> 00:51:14.704
Okay, well how about a text?

00:51:14.704 --> 00:51:16.306
Oh, well wait a minute.

00:51:16.306 --> 00:51:18.308 align:start size:84% position:16%
How many ways can
you interpret a text?

00:51:18.308 --> 00:51:21.178 align:start size:84% position:16%
Oh, a near infinite
number of ways.

00:51:21.178 --> 00:51:23.113
This is true, technically.

00:51:23.113 --> 00:51:24.681
A near infinite number of ways.

00:51:25.615 --> 00:51:27.084
Well, okay, which of those

00:51:28.185 --> 00:51:30.821 align:start size:84% position:16%
interpretations
should take priority?

00:51:30.821 --> 00:51:32.122
Well,

00:51:32.122 --> 00:51:34.224
we don't know how
we figure that out.

00:51:34.224 --> 00:51:35.725
And so well how do we know

00:51:35.725 --> 00:51:38.028
it's not just the naked
expression of power?

00:51:39.096 --> 00:51:40.764
Well, sometimes it is,

00:51:42.165 --> 00:51:45.402 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that that's the idea,
it's like, this is a problem.

00:51:45.402 --> 00:51:47.804
Now it's the wrong
solution because

00:51:47.804 --> 00:51:49.473
it isn't, the naked will to...

00:51:49.473 --> 00:51:50.974
The fundamental human motivation

00:51:50.974 --> 00:51:52.209
is not the naked will to power.

00:51:52.209 --> 00:51:54.444 align:start size:84% position:16%
Here's the fundamental
human motivation,

00:51:54.444 --> 00:51:55.245
it's...

00:51:58.181 --> 00:51:59.983 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'll tell you a quick
story, if you don't mind,

00:51:59.983 --> 00:52:01.918
hopefully it'll be quick.

00:52:01.918 --> 00:52:04.754 align:start position:0%
I went to see Die Meistersinger
in New York, the Opera.

00:52:05.956 --> 00:52:07.524
Okay, so it's very
cool, this Opera,

00:52:07.524 --> 00:52:09.926
I'm gonna write about it
in detail in this book.

00:52:09.926 --> 00:52:11.661
But here's the basic story.

00:52:11.661 --> 00:52:12.896
There's a town called Nurnberg,

00:52:12.896 --> 00:52:14.397 align:start size:84% position:16%
and there's a bunch
of guilds in the town.

00:52:14.397 --> 00:52:15.799
Trek, craftsman,

00:52:16.733 --> 00:52:18.001
cobblers

00:52:18.001 --> 00:52:19.402
like blacksmiths,

00:52:19.402 --> 00:52:21.905
guys who work, who
make things, right?

00:52:21.905 --> 00:52:23.707 align:start size:84% position:16%
So they're glued
right to the bottom,

00:52:23.707 --> 00:52:26.243
they know how to wire
the electrical outlet.

00:52:26.243 --> 00:52:29.079 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now they've all organized
together and elected

00:52:29.079 --> 00:52:32.315
a master singer in each guild.

00:52:32.315 --> 00:52:34.718
So the head of the guild,
isn't one of the craftsman,

00:52:34.718 --> 00:52:36.319
although he is a craftsman,

00:52:36.319 --> 00:52:37.954
he also has to be a singer.

00:52:39.356 --> 00:52:42.259 align:start size:97% position:3%
Okay, now all these guild guys
get together, the singers,

00:52:42.259 --> 00:52:44.294
and they elect each new singer.

00:52:45.462 --> 00:52:47.597
And so the story
is set up so that

00:52:48.732 --> 00:52:50.634 align:start size:84% position:16%
a new singer is going
to be elected and

00:52:50.634 --> 00:52:53.637 line:0%
one of the craftsman singers.

00:52:53.637 --> 00:52:57.240 line:0%
says he'll stake his entire
fortune and his daughter

00:52:57.240 --> 00:52:58.775 line:0%
on this new contest.

00:52:58.775 --> 00:53:01.478
He'll award his daughter
to the new master singer.

00:53:01.478 --> 00:53:03.413
Although she has a choice,

00:53:03.413 --> 00:53:04.714
she doesn't have to
do it by the way.

00:53:04.714 --> 00:53:07.017 align:start size:88% position:13%
So this isn't like
patriarchal oppression,

00:53:07.017 --> 00:53:09.853 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's a father trying to
offer his daughter the best

00:53:09.853 --> 00:53:12.422
after judicious consideration.

00:53:12.422 --> 00:53:14.791 align:start size:94% position:6%
Fully cognizant of the
fact that he loves her and

00:53:14.791 --> 00:53:17.661
allowing her choice,
encouraging her choice.

00:53:17.661 --> 00:53:19.029
A Knight comes in,

00:53:19.029 --> 00:53:20.797
who's wandered around
in the natural world,

00:53:20.797 --> 00:53:22.999
and he's such a
great singer that

00:53:22.999 --> 00:53:25.235
they can't help, but
recognize his talent,

00:53:25.235 --> 00:53:27.204
but he breaks all the rules,

00:53:27.204 --> 00:53:29.539
and the girl falls
in love with him.

00:53:29.539 --> 00:53:31.141
Okay, so,

00:53:31.141 --> 00:53:34.277
this guy who puts up
his fortune, he says,

00:53:36.947 --> 00:53:39.416 align:start size:97% position:3%
"'Cause people have criticized
me for only pursuing money,

00:53:39.416 --> 00:53:41.017 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm willing to
subordinate my fortune

00:53:41.017 --> 00:53:42.919
to the pursuit of
this highest art.

00:53:44.421 --> 00:53:46.823 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I'll put my daughter
on the line too."

00:53:46.823 --> 00:53:48.625
It's like, this is real.

00:53:48.625 --> 00:53:49.893
Okay, so the rest...

00:53:49.893 --> 00:53:51.895
Now, so the man all get
together in these guilds

00:53:51.895 --> 00:53:54.598 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they try to
elect the top figure,

00:53:54.598 --> 00:53:56.399
and this guy's name is...

00:53:59.336 --> 00:54:01.504 align:start size:94% position:6%
All right, the next thing
that happens is that

00:54:01.504 --> 00:54:03.273 align:start size:84% position:16%
the guilds degenerate
into competition

00:54:03.273 --> 00:54:05.875 align:start size:88% position:13%
because they're all
fighting for this girl.

00:54:05.875 --> 00:54:08.044 align:start size:81% position:19%
And that's what
happens among men.

00:54:08.044 --> 00:54:10.046
It's like they do get together,

00:54:10.046 --> 00:54:12.983 align:start size:97% position:3%
they do elect someone
whose highest amongst them,

00:54:12.983 --> 00:54:14.217 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then the
highest get together

00:54:14.217 --> 00:54:16.319
and elect what is highest yet.

00:54:16.319 --> 00:54:19.289
That's what unites
us, that's real.

00:54:19.289 --> 00:54:21.791 align:start size:88% position:13%
And we do fragment
because of competition.

00:54:21.791 --> 00:54:23.093
So that can fall apart.

00:54:23.093 --> 00:54:25.228
Anyways, the opera is
a masterful exploration

00:54:25.228 --> 00:54:26.263
of all of that.

00:54:26.263 --> 00:54:27.998
But it isn't will to power that

00:54:27.998 --> 00:54:30.000 align:start size:81% position:19%
is at the basis
of categorization.

00:54:30.000 --> 00:54:33.270 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's something like respect
for the divine word,

00:54:33.270 --> 00:54:35.605 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that's indistinguishable
from truth.

00:54:37.641 --> 00:54:40.744 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that's set against
the will of power,

00:54:40.744 --> 00:54:41.678
and it's way more...

00:54:41.678 --> 00:54:43.647
Try it out from your own life.

00:54:43.647 --> 00:54:46.616 align:start size:84% position:16%
Do things for power,
see what happens,

00:54:46.616 --> 00:54:48.218
that's hell.

00:54:48.218 --> 00:54:49.753
'Cause even if you
get what you want,

00:54:49.753 --> 00:54:52.222
by the time you get it,
you'll be a psychopath.

00:54:52.222 --> 00:54:54.424
No love, nothing.

00:54:54.424 --> 00:54:56.326 align:start size:84% position:16%
Just corruption
everywhere around you.

00:54:57.961 --> 00:55:00.430 align:start size:84% position:16%
Truth, that's a whole
different thing.

00:55:00.430 --> 00:55:03.400
And we're so powerful now,
we better tell the truth.

00:55:04.567 --> 00:55:06.870
We could have paradise
laid out in front of us,

00:55:06.870 --> 00:55:09.873 align:start size:94% position:6%
if we're careful or we
could let everything burn.

00:55:11.841 --> 00:55:14.311 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Do you think you've
made way on this?

00:55:14.311 --> 00:55:16.813 align:start size:97% position:3%
When you've talked, I
watched just about a week ago

00:55:16.813 --> 00:55:20.083
with your chat with Steven
Pinker and Jonathan Haidt,

00:55:20.083 --> 00:55:22.986
two guys who I hold
in the highest like
intellectual regard.

00:55:22.986 --> 00:55:25.855
I've had them on the show,
I like them both very much.

00:55:25.855 --> 00:55:28.725 align:start size:97% position:3%
They come from the more
atheist perspective on this,

00:55:28.725 --> 00:55:31.161
we've talked about
this a million times.

00:55:31.161 --> 00:55:32.195
- Yeah, so they think.

00:55:32.195 --> 00:55:34.664
- Right, okay, so that's it.

00:55:34.664 --> 00:55:35.899
- That's not, I'm
going to Cambridge-

00:55:35.899 --> 00:55:37.434 align:start size:84% position:16%
- That's where your
argument is that that-

00:55:37.434 --> 00:55:39.602
- I'm going Cambridge and
Oxford to talk about this,

00:55:39.602 --> 00:55:41.304
in detail, what I just laid out.

00:55:42.706 --> 00:55:45.675 align:start size:97% position:3%
'Cause what I wanna do is,
especially when I go to the UK

00:55:45.675 --> 00:55:48.044 align:start size:84% position:16%
in these conversations
is I wanna

00:55:48.044 --> 00:55:51.614 align:start size:94% position:6%
lay out a technical
argument for sacred depth.

00:55:51.614 --> 00:55:53.316
So imagine this,

00:55:53.316 --> 00:55:57.520
we have a intuition that
there's depth in literature

00:55:57.520 --> 00:55:59.456
'cause we think, well
that's a shallow story,

00:55:59.456 --> 00:56:00.690
and that's a deep
story and we think,

00:56:00.690 --> 00:56:03.493 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Well that was really
compelling and gripping.

00:56:03.493 --> 00:56:06.262 align:start size:88% position:13%
It hit me at a
profound level, right?"

00:56:06.262 --> 00:56:08.064 align:start size:94% position:6%
Versus, "Well, that was
just light entertainment."

00:56:08.064 --> 00:56:09.766
Light, right?

00:56:09.766 --> 00:56:12.068
We all have those intuitions.

00:56:12.068 --> 00:56:15.572
So what is this depth, and
how far to down does it go?

00:56:15.572 --> 00:56:18.007
Well, it goes down to
the bottom of things.

00:56:19.142 --> 00:56:20.643
What's at the bottom?

00:56:20.643 --> 00:56:23.947 align:start size:97% position:3%
Well what's at the bottom
is by definition, religious.

00:56:24.914 --> 00:56:26.349
And why would I say that?

00:56:26.349 --> 00:56:29.753 align:start size:97% position:3%
Well it's because when things
move you at that level,

00:56:29.753 --> 00:56:31.121
that's how you experience it.

00:56:31.121 --> 00:56:33.656
It's not thought,
it's way deeper.

00:56:33.656 --> 00:56:34.491
So it's like,

00:56:35.859 --> 00:56:38.328 align:start position:0%
I go out with my dock at
night, up north where I am now,

00:56:38.328 --> 00:56:40.964
and it's dark and the
expanse of the heavens

00:56:40.964 --> 00:56:42.665
is laid out above me.

00:56:42.665 --> 00:56:47.670
And I look up, well, I'm not
thinking religious thoughts,

00:56:48.805 --> 00:56:51.574
I'm confronting the
infinite itself,

00:56:51.574 --> 00:56:53.877
and that moves you inside.

00:56:53.877 --> 00:56:56.312
And I would say it even
moves you to imitation,

00:56:56.312 --> 00:56:57.547
because you know what it means

00:56:57.547 --> 00:56:59.516 align:start size:84% position:16%
to have your head
in the stars, right?

00:56:59.516 --> 00:57:01.217
I mean, it means
to be oriented up

00:57:01.217 --> 00:57:03.052
in the highest possible manner.

00:57:03.052 --> 00:57:05.288 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so we're so compelled
to imitate again

00:57:05.288 --> 00:57:07.724
at an instinctual level
that we can even imitate

00:57:07.724 --> 00:57:10.560 align:start position:0%
the infinite that we see spread
above us in the night sky.

00:57:10.560 --> 00:57:12.996
And that's a religious calling.

00:57:12.996 --> 00:57:14.964 align:start size:97% position:3%
And all this talk about
whether you believe in God,

00:57:14.964 --> 00:57:16.666
and it's all propositionalised.

00:57:16.666 --> 00:57:19.169
It's like, well it's
it misses the point.

00:57:20.370 --> 00:57:21.938
That's why I don't ever
answer when people say,

00:57:21.938 --> 00:57:23.173
